# Hostile audit — hull-augmentation outer-pocket mixed-wall repacking

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Audited source:** `research/target_c_outer_pocket_mixed_wall_repacking.md`  
**Verdict:** PASS at the stated Section-67/77 outer-pocket scope.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.

## 1. Geometry and scope

Canonical Section 67 constructs a 2-connected hull-augmented graph by adding force-hull
sides. Each new convex outer pocket inside `F` is bounded by one added side and one
complementary old outer-contact route. Canonical Section 67.6 counts the added side as
one graph-boundary edge and explicitly assigns contact-isolated source points on an
added side to the unique adjacent interior pocket.

This is not the geometry of the exterior caps in `K\setminus int F` from Sections
77.2/80/162. The source theorem now states that distinction explicitly. No conclusion
in this audit extends the mixed-wall theorem to those exterior caps.

## 2. Shield audit

A subunit segment from a safe replacement point to an old source outside the pocket
must first leave the convex pocket through one of three boundary types.

1. Relative interior of the added side: the replacement point is then at distance
   `<1` from that side, contradicting the side-distance guard.
2. A boundary source vertex: the vertex guard applies directly if the old point is the
   vertex; otherwise the two segment pieces have total length at least two.
3. Relative interior of an old unit-contact edge: source separation puts the outside
   point at distance at least one from both endpoints, and the exact two-endpoint
   crossing inequality forces the replacement point to be within unit distance of
   those endpoints.

A contact-isolated source point lying on the added side is protected by case 1; the
added side is never treated as a unit contact.

**Verdict:** the mixed-wall shield is valid.

## 3. Diameter-cover replacement

Assigned chamber points are contact-isolated and are outside the positive contact
core. Actual diameter endpoints are loaded force-hull/source-hull extreme vertices,
so the assigned set is disjoint from any diameter-graph vertex cover.

Deleting the assigned chamber points and a cover of the complete actual diameter
graph, then inserting the same total number of safe points, preserves cardinality and
unit separation. Every new point lies in `int F subset int K`, so every pair involving
a new point has distance strictly below `D`; the cover removes every old-old pair at
exactly `D`.

**Verdict:** the strict-diameter competitor is valid.

## 4. Capacity constants

For an outer pocket with `ell` old unit-contact edges and added-side length `s`, the
augmented boundary count is `k=ell+1`. Hence

\[
 r=\sigma-k=A/a_0+s/2-\ell/2-z.
\]

The contact-chain area theorem gives safe area at least

\[
 A-s-\ell c_*-h_*.
\]

Dividing by `a_0=sqrt(3)/2` and substituting the preceding identity yields

\[
 {area(S_{mix})\over a_0}-z
 \ge r-{\pi\over3\sqrt3}\ell
 -\left({2\over\sqrt3}+{1\over2}\right)s
 -{h_*\over a_0}.
\]

A single triangular-lattice translate realizes at least the floor of the left-hand
side in extra packing points. The direction is a constructive lower bound, not a
reversal of Oler.

**Verdict:** the coefficients and floor criterion are correct.

## 5. Scope correction recorded

An earlier scratch file on this research branch used the phrase "exterior cap" for
this one-added-side geometry. That wording was rejected after checking Sections 77.2,
80 and 162. The valid object is the Section-67/77 hull-augmentation outer pocket inside
`F`. The final proof package must exclude the stale overbroad scratch file.

**Final verdict:** PASS for hull-augmentation outer pockets only. The Section-80/162
exterior-cap alternative still relies on the audited-working full-collar theorem of
Section 165.
